From ce72f77cb0ec4e2e74317a5b33340782e152f782 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chandler Newman <chandler2newman@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:01:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: record the context made current by creating one

Creating a context creates its first sub-context, which makes that
sub-context's GL context current, but vrend_state still named the
context that was current before. Switching back to that one was then
skipped as a no-op, and its next command ran in the new context's GL
context: its vertex array objects do not exist there and no framebuffer
is bound, so the draw fails with GL_INVALID_OPERATION and the context
is marked in error.

Any guest process that creates a GL context while another is rendering
triggers it, and a readback through TRANSFER_FROM_HOST_3D is where it
shows, because that path checks for GL errors.
---
 src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
index 2eb5bd3..2445fb7 100644
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
@@ -8044,6 +8044,14 @@ struct vrend_context *vrend_create_context(int id, uint32_t nlen, const char *de
    vrend_renderer_create_sub_ctx(grctx, 0);
    vrend_renderer_set_sub_ctx(grctx, 0);
 
+   /* Creating the sub-context made its GL context current. Record that, or
+    * the context that was current before still appears to be, and switching
+    * back to it is skipped: its next command would run in this context's GL
+    * context, against objects that do not exist there.
+    */
+   vrend_state.current_ctx = grctx;
+   vrend_state.current_hw_ctx = grctx;
+
    int glver = get_glsl_version();
    if (glver < 0) {
       virgl_error("Unable to query GL version\n");
-- 
2.53.0

